.TH STOP-MU2EDAQ-BIGREDBOX.SH 1 "2026-10-06" "mu2edaq-bigredbox 2.0.0" "Mu2e DAQ Manual"
.SH NAME
stop\-mu2edaq\-bigredbox.sh, stop_daq_alert.sh \- stop the Big Red Box alert listener
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B stop\-mu2edaq\-bigredbox.sh
.RI [ pid_file ]
.br
.B stop_daq_alert.sh
.RI [ pid_file ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Control\-room stop script, run as
.BR "crs\-app stop bigredbox" .
.B stop_daq_alert.sh
is a git symbolic link to this script.
.PP
The PID is read from
.IR pid_file .
If that file does not exist, the script asks
.BR lsof (8)
for the processes holding UDP port
.B CRS_PORT_UDP
and takes the first whose command line matches
.B mu2edaq[\-_]bigredbox
or
.BR daq_alert ,
so a listener started by hand, or one whose PID file was removed, can still be
stopped; an unrelated port holder is never signalled.
It then sends
.BR SIGTERM ,
waits up to
.B CRS_STOP_TIMEOUT
seconds, sends
.B SIGKILL
if the process is still alive, and removes the PID file.
.PP
On Linux two listeners can hold the port at once (see
.BR mu2edaq\-bigredbox (1),
.BR "Single instance" );
the PID file then names only the newer one, and only that one is stopped.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.I pid_file
PID file to read.
Default
.IR /tmp/daq_alert.pid .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /tmp/daq_alert.pid
Default PID file, written by the listener.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CRS_PORT_UDP
Port used for the
.BR lsof (8)
fallback; default 37020.
.TP
.B CRS_STOP_TIMEOUT
Seconds to wait after
.B SIGTERM
before
.BR SIGKILL ;
default 10.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Stopped, or not running (no PID file and no matching port holder, or a stale
PID).
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS 4
.nf
\&./stop\-mu2edaq\-bigredbox.sh
\&./stop\-mu2edaq\-bigredbox.sh ~/daq_alert.pid
ss \-ulpn 'sport = :37020'      # Linux: confirm nothing is left
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR start\-mu2edaq\-bigredbox.sh (1),
.BR mu2edaq\-bigredbox (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Mu2e DAQ Group, Fermi National Accelerator Laboratory.
